ets  so a couple more details on our data  entry process  during data entry shifts  lots and lots of volunteers would scour  state and u.s territory dashboards  then everyone would write the findings  into a giant google sheet that i showed  you earlier  which we called worksheet2  no one talks about worksheet one  there's also lots and lots of metadata  attached to every state and everything  is ultimately stored in a bunch of  interconnected sheets  so in general  like many other aspects of the kova  tracking project  work and organization this process very  much had to evolve as we went uh we  you know did a lot of building the plane  uh as we were flying it  because change is the only constant  um  so google sheets is fantastic in many  ways uh it's an awesome tool for  collaborative data entry um especially  for data structured as a table which  ours was  it's generic and flexible enough to  accommodate folks with different  backgrounds and kind of different levels  of tech savvy  and respond to you know different  circumstances like lots of types of data  changes which  came up a lot um and lastly the the  tabular format of our data  was very much baked into our data entry  process and pipelines  excuse me uh every step of the way  so all of that meant that for a long  time google sheets was our world for  anything we needed  and we had pretty awesome tooling within  sheets to make all of these things  possible  this was a mix of google apps script  magic and lots of excel type power usage  and all this powered very kind of domain  specific data collection and published  processes  so using sheets for data entry was  awesome  the problem was that in the beginning we  were also using it as a data store for  time series data which was less awesome  because we started running into a bunch  of issues  so first of all our data just got too  big  we were adding 56 rows  and an increasing number of columns at  least once a day  and because the project ran uh way way 