eflected  maybe only in a visual version of the  website as opposed to something like a  data csv or metadata  um in addition to that screenshots also  helped us maintain history uh so this  was particularly useful for  situations where we needed to update our  data um in response to retroactive state  data revisions which happened all the  time um and having older screenshots uh  helped us keep track of what the states  themselves were putting out  and then lastly we did this for archival  reasons so eventually we want to be able  to present a record  of what was reported and when and to  have that record be as complete as  possible  so this is a quick tech overview of the  system that we made  which captures state websites and does a  little bit of sneaky scripting to  navigate to the right data points  uh so in a nutshell we had a python  script  which ran as a crown job several times a  day  to collect screenshots um  i first ran on a personal laptop  and then at some point we decided that  we needed to be grown-ups uh and moved  it to an amazon ec2 instance um so the  script used a cloud service called  phantom.js cloud uh to render websites  and capture screenshots um and also it  let us  phantom gs cloud supported various  custom javascripts to navigate around  before taking a snapshot more on that in  a second  so all these customizations  ultimately lived in yammo configs on  github in general we were a very  github-centered org all of our code is  actually still up on there um  and then ultimately we stored  screenshots on s3 and we made them  accessible on our website um  we found that python lends itself really  well to this kind of thing most of our  code was in python  it's easy to quickly write readable  scripts and there's good library support  also  for things like s3 and and also slack  integration it's not pictured here but  we integrated with slack to alert us to  screenshot failures  so this is an example of a state  screenshot config  it's structured to our use cases so has  a url some web browser settings like  page size  and a message that we use to remind  ourselves uh roughly what this config is  doing and why we needed it in the first  place um the interesting part here is  this bit in the middle called the  overseer script um this example is  definitely not the most complicated  thing we had to do but really often we  would need to do stuff like  click on the fifth tab of this dashboard  which of course doesn't have a reliable  selector name because why would it so  we click on the fifth html element of  this type  then weigh 49.3 seconds for the page to  load click her heels spin around three  times and hope for the best um  a lot of the time this worked uh but  sometimes it didn't  and in cases where it did not one of our  awesome volunteers would take a  screenshot manually and put it into our  screenshot store  so along the way we ended up  basically building an accidental brain  trust of automatically navigating around  arcgis  and power bi dashboards  um i just want to say here this seems an  awful lot like automating data  collection but for the reasons i  mentioned before  where things kept changing  we couldn't fully replace our human  processes  we considered it at multiple points and  decided against it so screenshots were  ultimately a best effort project um  where our competency around uh scraping  power bi  lagged behind our core competency around  coded data collection  at the bottom that's just i'm just  pasting the github repo for our  screenshots  code and customizations um feel free to  poke around any other repos that we have  on there as well  so now i want to talk about data storage  um  as i said the the core tool for data  entry for our volunteers was google  sheets um so let's say now we have all  of this data that we collect very  carefully with our eyes and our hands  uh where does it go  so this part of the talk is  going to involve a lot of our feelings  about google sheets  so a couple more details on our data  entry process  during data entry shifts  lots and lots of volunteers would scour  state and u.s territory dashboards  then everyone would write the findings  into a giant google sheet that i showed  you earlier  which we called worksheet2  no one talks about worksheet one  there's also lots and lots of metadata  attached to every state and everything  is ultimately stored in a bunch of  interconnected sheets  so in general  like many other aspects of the kova  tracking project  work and organization this process very  much had to evolve as we went uh we  you know did a lot of building the plane  uh as we were flying it  because change is the only constant  um  so google sheets is fantastic in many  ways uh it's an awesome tool for  collaborative data entry um especially  for data structured as a table which  ours was  it's generic and flexible enough to  accommodate folks with different  backgrounds and kind of different levels  of tech savvy  and respond to you know different  circumstances like lots of types of data  changes which  came up a lot um and lastly the the  tabular format of our data  was very much baked into our data entry  process and pipelines  excuse me uh every step of the way  so all of that meant that for a long  time google sheets was our world for  anything we needed  and we had pretty awesome tooling within  sheets to make all of these things  possible  this was a mix of google apps script  magic and lots of excel type power usage  and all this powered very kind of domain  specific data collection and published  processes  so using sheets for data entry was  awesome  the problem was that in the beginning we  were also using it as a data store for  time series data which was less awesome  because we started running into a bunch  of issues  so first of all our data just got too  big  we were adding 56 rows  and an increasing number of columns at  least once a day  and because the project ran uh way way  way longer than the few weeks that we  originally thought that we would need to  do this far  this ended up pretty quickly  so the performance of google sheets  started degrading with this much data  and this many people trying to edit it  at the same time  we also ran into issues around  accidentally losing edit history  if the cell got deleted and with a lot  of volunteers this kind of thing is  bound to happen at some point  rolling back mistakes was hard because  all of our tooling was dependencies  coded within the space of sheets  and  problems like this were also getting  harder to prevent within sheets because  as our data got more complex uh so did  the problem of validating it  so we ended up adding a database layer  that took a bunch of the data store and  data validation pressure off of google  sheets  the challenge here was adding a critical  piece of infrastructure in the middle of  a production data pipeline  basically amounted to  building one plane while you're flying  it and then building a second plane  while you're flying it and trying to  hook it up  to the first plane mid-flight with no  interruptions um so those kinds of  changes are never easy but it was worth  it for the separation of concerns  between  data entry and data storage  so some brief a brief overview of our  stack  on the left  we set up a postgres database on amazon  rds and the data in this database became  our published source of truth  we also had an internal api layer  which i'll talk about more in a minute  but ultimately it supported both reads  and writes and it was the only  programmatic route to the database so  this was a python flask gap that was  heavily built on top of sql alchemy  that sat between the database and our  spreadsheets  and this api layer took care of  transforming and validating incoming  data  both from our kind of main data entry  sheet and also other sheets and scripts  that  that we built over time to support the  data editing process um and this  internal api would also transform the  data to feedback out to our website and  our public data api which i'll talk  about a little bit later  then i also wanted to say a couple of  things about the way that we represented  our data um so first of all each metric  was its own data column  it's very straightforward it's easy to  visually scan less error prone to query  that way  and it's also similar to the mental data  model that we kind of collectively built  up over time  so this let us keep the data store  itself  as simple as possible  and then second in order to preserve  history we set it up so the data was  never modified it was only added which i  think is a fairly common approach  but the way that we  would add the data is through batches  so every single data row was attached to  a batch id  um and we could interpret those  chronologically and essentially play it  forward as needed um so this borrows a  little bit from the idea of database  transaction logging  except applied on the data modeling  level  so for example if we had to go back and  revise our data  for some state and some date  in response to a state data revision we  would then know that the newer batch  higher batch id is the data that we  should treat as  currently accurate but since we start  all of it we could also trace these  changes over time  so flask and sql alchemy worked really  well for us here  to let us enforce correctness both on  incoming and outgoing data um  as part of this kind of internal api  layer that did all of the heavy lifting  on data transformation and semantics  so this method our columns are simple uh  our rows are simple and associated with  batches  and then the the last thing that i want  to note here um  we chose to take complexity out of sql  queries in this case um that would have  been another option for enforcing data  and variance um but instead we decided  to keep our sql queries simple and then  move that kind of logic largely into  python  where we could iterate and filter  through  uh all of our data in a way that was  more readable uh less error-prone  and also easier to uni-test our logic  which was pretty important  so overall keeping the data store as  simple as possible was our friend in  this uh and having a model that was a  combination of  simple data representation but then a  layer that would transform it as  necessary for the different use cases  work pretty well um and it's kind of a  more general point uh all of our tech at  ctp ended up being  on one hand generic solutions  on the other hand pretty custom and  scrappy um but on the third hand we  still have things like unit tests and  code reviews so uh from the engineering  standpoint um this was a pretty cool  balance to strike  and on the right here this uh this  became an emblem of our database project  with spreadsheets and data and data  this was a brilliant work by one of our  volunteers  so the last thing that i want to talk  about a little is how we shared and  contextualized the data that we  collected  so i mentioned before that we had a  public-facing data api um this went  through several iterations over the  lifetime of the project  v0 was straight up a public google sheet  that reflected the structure of how we  collected the data  once that stopped scaling which was five  minutes later  we built a v1 of the api which  essentially served out  flat csvs and uh simple json trials  these were basically just key value  pairs for  everest it was testing and outcomes  metrics and then it was all of our data  and then eventually we  fairly laid into the project but we put  out a v2 of the public api which was  structured json files  um so we used what we had learned about  the data over time to organize it a  little bit better so things like nesting  related metrics like grouping all tests  together into a top-level test category  um and then breaking that out further  into like the type of cova test reform  like pcr and antigen and so on and so  forth um it also let us  add things like derived values like  smoothing the data over the last seven  days um  structure the metadata a little bit  better and metadata was a big part of  the project as well  um  so on and so forth uh  so v1 and v2 of this public api  these were effectively a caching layer  on top of our data  that was backed by github static files  so easy to store and serve  scalability was a big concern for us  given that we were eventually fielding  millions of requests per day for this  data  so aside from making the data itself  available um our group tried our best to  use the experience of staring at these  numbers every day  to contextualize the shifting data  definitions and the data itself um  we were not data modelers or  epidemiologists  but distinguishing true anomalies from  data artifacts became a really important  part of what we try to communicate  so as an example of something where  context was really important  a state might suddenly receive a large  data dump of positive cases  which would not actually reflect that  day's data  but it would appear as a scary spike on  the time series um  or a state might change uh the  definition of some metric like how  exactly cases were being counted um so  overall the science communication and  editorial components of the cover  tracking project  became really important to us over time  we published daily tweets giving a data  update with context as needed  and a lot of blog posts a lot a lot of  blog posts going into more detail about  the data that we are working with those  are all still up on our website  covertracking.com  so  ctp was a completely incredible effort  to participate in um eventually our data  came to be used by a ton of people and  organizations um recited a lot of papers  and news stories and even some  congressional reports which was  pretty amazing  seeing policy changes as the result of  making this data available was an  incredible thing  um so this was a  really profound learning experience to  be part of this org for me at least i  think for all of us but one of the many  reasons for that is um  as we grew everything had to scale at  once um the number of volunteers and our  processes and our tooling  and all the things um so i i just want  to end with some data related takeaways  that have been helpful kind of for me to  think about and carry forward um so  first it's very very important to stay  connected to the data  um  data without context is meaningless uh  lying with data is very easy  and depending on the the nature of what  you're working with um you  you have to get your hands dirty and  keep them dirty in a sense  to trust what you're putting out into  the world  it's also important to think about data  storage and representation uh as early  as possible in the lifetime of a project  um infrastructure decisions that are  made early on will strongly influence  what happens later  both  in terms of technology and in terms of  process  in our case the early choice of google  sheets had a pretty strong and  persistent impact on our workflow  that sort of thing is tricky to change  later  but also it's important to be flexible  um so in a project like this where  things change all the time  we had to be willing to adapt our  processes and our tooling  which is also related to not automating  things too quickly  automation is a really good goal to have  but it's one where you need to learn as  much as possible about  the space that you're operating in  in order to make sure that you're  automating the right things at the right  time  so very very very importantly um ctp  went from just a handful of folks in the  early days to  over 800 volunteers in data entry data  quality  outreach reporting infrastructure  communication visualization the website  other teams that i'm not even  remembering right now but people donated  uh 20 000 hours of time  just in data entry alone  and i'm  incredibly grateful for the heart and  soul that went into this project  and the ways that people helped each  other out um  by the nature of the work that we were  doing this was a really intense time for  many people um we're watching and  collecting numbers at the same time as  everyone was also living through it  but we didn't want the organization to  be a great place to show up to every day  so people found  a ton of ways to be creative and kind  and funny  like that database emoji that i showed  you earlier  we actually had a really strong emoji  culture at ctp but um  either way this work would not have been  possible without this community i'm  really grateful to have been a part of  it and a giant thank you to all of our  volunteers who made this possible  thank you so much for listening happy to  take any questions you might have  also feel free to reach out to me on  twitter as well  thank you so much julia what an  interesting talk um  i'm watching here the chat for uh  questions any questions if not i have  one this is a joint project that was  rapidly evolving and  how did you govern this project  so you mean like what my personal  connection was no i mean like how do you  govern a project that evolves that  quickly  with that much data involved that much  web scraping  uh  sorry do you mean like how you  uh sorry i'm not actually not sure i  heard your question yeah like how how  often do you meet how do you do this  like who makes decisions i say i see so  uh the i mean honestly the decision  framework on this project was also  something that very much evolved over  time um i think when it was when it was  just a few of us you know everything was  i mean things were happening really  quickly everyone was kind of doing  everything like  um i initially started as just you know  doing data entry on the project too to  kind of get a sense of what we were even  doing  um  eventually uh not even really that  eventually but the project gained a lot  of volunteers really fast and so um we  we ended up  structuring our processes at the same  time as we also started structuring our  teams  and i would say that every team  depending on the function within ctp  had a different cadence for meetings and  how work was structured so um so a team  like data entry which also had the most  volunteers that was you know that was by  far the thing that needed like the  largest number of people in the largest  set of eyes and human time on it um  their work shifts were incredibly  structured and you know people would  sign up for shifts in advance um we  eventually came up with a training  program like we had data checkers we had  double checkers and like that was  structured and at the same time every  day until the shift was over and whereas  on a project like infrastructure  a lot of that was  i mean that that probably looked a  little bit more like uh um you know if a  startup starts remote first and everyone  just sort of figures it out as we go  along uh the cadence of work there uh  you know we didn't have to have shifts  but  we like instituted code reviews we  instituted sort of like the basic  building blocks of how do we all write a  bunch of code together without  everything crashing at the same time um  so so yeah i would say i would say that  varied uh quite a bit between teams  okay and i have to apologize here i just  saw that there are uh one million okay  less than one million a lot of questions  so um so i'm gonna go with uh them in  order of arrival here adam s who  initiated the project in terms of  leadership and authority  so  the project came about because there  were there were two people that had  known each other for a long time  that  both started putting together google  spreadsheets without knowing that each  other was doing it uh and then they  connected about it and found out that  this was a thing they were both tracking  so  alexis madrigal who's a journalist in  the atlantic and uh jeff hammerbacher  who's done a ton of work in 